1
00:00:00,960 --> 00:00:03,140
Movie and gadget lovers beware.

2
00:00:03,500 --> 00:00:06,240
If you're into the high-tech stuff of dreams you see in theaters,

3
00:00:06,680 --> 00:00:10,540
buckle your belts for a thrill ride beyond the big screen into reality.

4
00:00:11,260 --> 00:00:14,000
Super toys from fictional films actually exist

5
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:16,320
and may soon come to a retailer near you.

6
00:00:16,860 --> 00:00:21,180
Cars that fly and drive themselves to spiffy spy tools with X-ray vision.

7
00:00:21,860 --> 00:00:25,580
Now, the greatest movie gadgets, next on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,900
What's that?

9
00:00:40,540 --> 00:00:42,240
Some sort of a war tank.

10
00:00:42,820 --> 00:00:46,120
Think for a moment about the coolest stuff you could ever imagine.

11
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:49,140
Movie makers have been doing it for years.

12
00:00:49,800 --> 00:00:50,840
Make that decades.

13
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:59,360
Motion picture serials that played in theaters 50 years ago were way ahead of their time.

14
00:00:59,361 --> 00:01:02,720
I still can't believe that this little machine can predict earthquakes.

15
00:01:03,140 --> 00:01:04,860
Not only predicts, but prevents them.

16
00:01:05,520 --> 00:01:10,260
One imaginative director, who must have been an ancestor of film director James Cameron,

17
00:01:10,620 --> 00:01:14,840
came up with the idea of a futuristic submarine exploring worlds unknown.

18
00:01:16,520 --> 00:01:18,220
We're down 2,500 feet.

19
00:01:18,640 --> 00:01:21,860
Good, another 2,000 feet and I can stop the groundbreaking rain.

20
00:01:22,160 --> 00:01:23,300
Oh, you're all mad.

21
00:01:24,040 --> 00:01:27,620
If we go any deeper, the submarine will crush like the shell of an egg.

22
00:01:29,360 --> 00:01:32,780
Cameron took the idea to new depths in the abyss.

23
00:01:34,880 --> 00:01:35,600
Touchdown!

24
00:01:36,300 --> 00:01:41,580
In British Columbia, a world-renowned undersea explorer is doing it for real.

25
00:01:42,300 --> 00:01:43,000
We're trapped!

26
00:01:43,780 --> 00:01:48,960
Another filmmaking magician conjured up the notion of the gun of all guns.

27
00:01:50,080 --> 00:01:53,180
At the time, the weapon was called a disintegrator.

28
00:01:55,600 --> 00:01:58,540
In the fifth element, it was the ZF-1.

29
00:01:59,360 --> 00:02:03,980
In field tests being conducted right now, it's the XM-29.

30
00:02:10,080 --> 00:02:11,420
We're taking off.

31
00:02:11,820 --> 00:02:17,040
And what would the world of movie-dom be without all those fabulous vehicles that fly?

32
00:02:20,560 --> 00:02:24,860
Flash Gordon hardly ever lost a space race in his personal rocket ship.

33
00:02:25,140 --> 00:02:25,840
Flash.

34
00:02:26,640 --> 00:02:27,400
What is it, Zach?

35
00:02:27,401 --> 00:02:31,860
I've sighted a spaceship. It's one of Ming's ships from Mongo.

36
00:02:32,320 --> 00:02:35,940
In Chitty Chitty Bang Bang, the need for speed lost out to cute.

37
00:02:36,420 --> 00:02:41,700
It's the most phantasmagorical musical entertainment in the history of everything.

38
00:02:42,060 --> 00:02:46,840
But fast is roaring back with the first real flying car.

39
00:02:48,140 --> 00:02:51,620
Movies today are both trendsetters and trend followers.

40
00:02:51,621 --> 00:02:58,620
Fabulous gadgets we see on the silver screen are inspiring a whole new generation of inventors to think out of the box.

41
00:02:58,621 --> 00:03:04,620
But other screen gems have actually evolved from marvelous machines that have been around for years.

42
00:03:06,620 --> 00:03:08,620
The movie is XXX.

43
00:03:08,621 --> 00:03:12,620
And the techno star is a watercraft called Ahab.

44
00:03:12,621 --> 00:03:14,620
This is no mere boat.

45
00:03:14,621 --> 00:03:19,620
By order of director Rob Cohen, it had to look space-age cool.

46
00:03:19,621 --> 00:03:24,440
travel at a speed of 100 knots, and fire missiles while it's moving.

47
00:03:25,940 --> 00:03:30,560
We had several meetings, and out of one of the meetings, it came out like,

48
00:03:30,980 --> 00:03:34,460
okay, Rob, do you want this thing to work?

49
00:03:34,600 --> 00:03:38,040
Is it a motion picture prop? Is it going to be towed by another boat?

50
00:03:38,500 --> 00:03:39,940
Which direction do we want to take this?

51
00:03:40,200 --> 00:03:43,920
And he said, I want the real deal. I want this to work.

52
00:03:43,921 --> 00:03:46,180
And with that, I said, okay, no problem.

53
00:03:46,181 --> 00:03:50,000
immediately going outside and throwing up, you know.

54
00:03:50,860 --> 00:03:53,680
Rob Cohen wanted the climax of his movie

55
00:03:53,681 --> 00:03:57,420
to be Ahab flying down the Vltava River in the Czech Republic,

56
00:03:57,980 --> 00:04:01,580
with actor Vin Diesel aboard just before the vessel explodes.

57
00:04:05,220 --> 00:04:06,880
Three minutes to target.

58
00:04:06,881 --> 00:04:20,881
For a watercraft to believably move at the high speeds needed for XXX, it had to be,

59
00:04:21,040 --> 00:04:23,840
or at least look like, a hydrofoil.

60
00:04:23,841 --> 00:04:30,100
A boat that doesn't travel in the water, but above it, lifting up onto wing-shaped foils

61
00:04:30,101 --> 00:04:35,720
to reduce friction and drag.

62
00:04:35,721 --> 00:04:39,920
Real-life hydrofoils have actually existed for a long time.

63
00:04:39,921 --> 00:04:45,060
The first flight was recorded by a boatmaker in Chicago in 1894.

64
00:04:45,061 --> 00:04:51,900
Since that time, hydrofoil designs have evolved for pleasure, ferry, passenger, and military

65
00:04:51,901 --> 00:04:52,900
use.

66
00:04:52,901 --> 00:04:57,780
For more than 50 years, the U.S. Navy experimented with the use of hydrofoils.

67
00:04:57,781 --> 00:05:04,520
In the early 1980s, the Navy built and commissioned six hydrofoil patrol boats, but they were decommissioned

68
00:05:04,521 --> 00:05:09,760
ten years later too expensive and troublesome to maintain

69
00:05:09,761 --> 00:05:14,660
so the problem for triple X production designers was not an issue of building

70
00:05:14,661 --> 00:05:21,260
something that had never been built but making it look futuristically cool more

71
00:05:21,261 --> 00:05:28,960
like a rocket and being able to build it for the time and money in the budget

72
00:05:28,961 --> 00:05:36,500
When John Frazier first contacted established hydrofoil builders, he discovered another

73
00:05:36,501 --> 00:05:37,500
obstacle.

74
00:05:37,501 --> 00:05:43,900
The firms had liability concerns about a watercraft that would basically be experimental, but

75
00:05:43,901 --> 00:05:48,200
expected to be safe and reliable at very high speeds.

76
00:05:48,201 --> 00:05:51,980
A lot of people just did not want to talk to us when you tell us that there's going

77
00:05:51,981 --> 00:05:53,400
to be stunt guys riding on it.

78
00:05:53,401 --> 00:05:56,300
Got a big-name actor riding on top of this thing.

79
00:05:56,480 --> 00:06:01,240
And that's when the phone goes clunk, because they don't want the liability.

80
00:06:02,260 --> 00:06:04,980
John Frazier had a classic Hollywood dilemma.

81
00:06:05,500 --> 00:06:09,160
A director asking for the moon, and no way to give it to him.

82
00:06:09,800 --> 00:06:17,760
XXX production designer Gavin Bouquet knew that Rob Cohen would not be satisfied with anything less than a very real Ahab.

83
00:06:17,761 --> 00:06:20,760
somehow, someway

84
00:06:20,761 --> 00:06:24,140
he and Frasier had to come up with a working water vessel

85
00:06:24,141 --> 00:06:24,960
to fill the bill

86
00:06:24,961 --> 00:06:28,540
or they would both be non-working movie makers

87
00:06:28,541 --> 00:06:32,820
Rob was very keen to have as much of the real Ahab there

88
00:06:32,821 --> 00:06:34,140
in physical form as possible

89
00:06:34,141 --> 00:06:36,160
we have a lot of action with Vin Diesel

90
00:06:36,161 --> 00:06:37,880
on top of the Ahab at the end of the film

91
00:06:37,881 --> 00:06:39,220
so he felt it was really critical

92
00:06:39,221 --> 00:06:42,100
to actually get Vin on the water on Ahab

93
00:06:42,101 --> 00:06:45,680
he was just very keen to have Vin in the river in Prague

94
00:06:45,681 --> 00:06:47,460
for the final sequence of the film

95
00:06:47,461 --> 00:06:51,420
and for the Ahab to be seen to be traveling on the river in a real situation.

96
00:06:52,580 --> 00:06:58,460
Slowly, over many sleepless nights and nightmares about a real Ahab exploding too soon,

97
00:06:58,920 --> 00:07:00,840
Frazier and Bouquet came up with a plan.

98
00:07:02,320 --> 00:07:06,240
A fake, full-size Ahab was built out of metal and fiberglass.

99
00:07:07,240 --> 00:07:12,980
Then, marine movie effects expert Ransom Walrud mounted it on a 24-foot skiff,

100
00:07:13,680 --> 00:07:16,920
basically a fishing boat powered by two outboard motors.

101
00:07:17,460 --> 00:07:19,287
So, in one sense, it is practical.

102
00:07:19,288 --> 00:07:25,360
It was on the water, and it was going more or less at the speed that you see it in most of the shots on the film.

103
00:07:25,361 --> 00:07:31,260
And Ransom was very clever in terms of the structure that he attached to Ahab that connected to the skiff,

104
00:07:31,360 --> 00:07:37,860
because it threw up a lot of spray, as it should do, that almost visually covered most of the skiff.

105
00:07:39,160 --> 00:07:42,080
Almost covering the skiff, of course, wasn't good enough.

106
00:07:42,081 --> 00:07:49,080
That's where digital effects designer Joel Hynek of Digital Domain came into the picture.

107
00:07:49,081 --> 00:07:54,080
Frazier and Bouquet had put a boat in the river with a rocket on top.

108
00:07:54,081 --> 00:07:59,080
Now, Joel had to make it fly.

109
00:07:59,081 --> 00:08:05,080
My main worry was getting rid of the boat, the sled they called it,

110
00:08:05,081 --> 00:08:11,080
that was underneath the Ahab model on the river for a lot of shots.

111
00:08:11,081 --> 00:08:18,080
After several trial and error experiments, the answer came in the form of digitally enhanced water.

112
00:08:18,081 --> 00:08:25,080
The first step was to remove the sled and then replace it with water.

113
00:08:25,081 --> 00:08:32,080
And you're able to take the water and stretch it over an area that was in front of the sled

114
00:08:32,081 --> 00:08:36,080
to where it met the part of the sled that we were going to keep.

115
00:08:36,081 --> 00:08:39,940
And then we added some spray elements that we shot in our parking lot

116
00:08:39,941 --> 00:08:44,560
and other spray elements to cover over the area that we had just stretched.

117
00:08:52,560 --> 00:08:57,820
When we built the Ahab, we didn't realize how great the digital guys were going to make that thing look.

118
00:08:58,320 --> 00:09:01,120
When you see it going down the water in Prague,

119
00:09:01,620 --> 00:09:05,200
and then when you see it on the screen, it's like two different elements.

120
00:09:05,820 --> 00:09:10,000
They did an absolute superb job of getting rid of the boat that was below it

121
00:09:10,001 --> 00:09:12,380
and adding the spray of the water in the right places,

122
00:09:12,560 --> 00:09:14,420
and I was real impressed with what they did with it.

123
00:09:17,500 --> 00:09:19,220
Welcome to the Sanchez Order!

124
00:09:22,100 --> 00:09:27,860
So the story of making Ahab had a classic happy and explosive Hollywood ending.

125
00:09:28,400 --> 00:09:33,020
The shots looked terrific, and the offense designers kept their jobs.

126
00:09:33,940 --> 00:09:41,120
For those triple-X lovers who viewed the notion of hydrofoiling as a dream only achievable in the movies, take heart.

127
00:09:41,980 --> 00:09:46,740
In the real world, enjoying flight a few feet above water is within your grasp.

128
00:09:47,740 --> 00:09:52,300
If you're willing to sacrifice the rocket ship look of Ahab for something more traditional,

129
00:09:52,860 --> 00:09:55,380
here's the man and the boat to watch.

130
00:09:55,381 --> 00:10:00,740
Harry Larson of Seattle, Washington

131
00:10:00,741 --> 00:10:03,460
had been an avid boating enthusiast for years

132
00:10:03,461 --> 00:10:06,180
when he decided to convert his cabin cruiser

133
00:10:06,181 --> 00:10:09,020
into, you guessed it, a hyperfoil

134
00:10:09,021 --> 00:10:12,460
He didn't have any conversion kits

135
00:10:12,461 --> 00:10:14,380
not even any prefab parts

136
00:10:14,381 --> 00:10:16,740
but he did have an engineering background

137
00:10:16,741 --> 00:10:19,160
that enabled him to determine what he needed

138
00:10:19,161 --> 00:10:20,960
to make his own boat fly

139
00:10:20,961 --> 00:10:25,140
What you see here is the bow strut and foil

140
00:10:25,141 --> 00:10:25,980
with a hydrofoil.

141
00:10:26,420 --> 00:10:28,540
The strut is made of stainless steel.

142
00:10:28,820 --> 00:10:30,860
The foil is high-density fiberglass.

143
00:10:31,460 --> 00:10:34,040
The strut at the moment is turned a bit.

144
00:10:34,340 --> 00:10:36,460
That's how the boat is steered.

145
00:10:37,880 --> 00:10:42,440
The front or bow foil is used to control the height of a hydrofoil.

146
00:10:43,200 --> 00:10:44,860
Like the ailerons of a flight wing,

147
00:10:45,220 --> 00:10:47,200
they remain underwater when horizontal.

148
00:10:47,720 --> 00:10:49,540
But when Harry pulls back the throttle,

149
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:53,560
they angle up, just like airplane wings, to produce lift.

150
00:10:55,140 --> 00:11:01,400
The back or aft foil contains the power source, a 30-inch extension of an outboard motor,

151
00:11:02,400 --> 00:11:08,640
and, like the tail of an airplane, the sophisticated electronics to keep this flying ship stable.

152
00:11:10,280 --> 00:11:19,440
For Harry, however, all the sophisticated stuff came out of a homemade software program running below deck on a laptop computer.

153
00:11:19,441 --> 00:11:28,680
This is a submerged foil hydrofoil, which implies that it has no ability to maintain its roll state,

154
00:11:28,840 --> 00:11:34,520
in other words, stay upright, without a computer or some other device to do that.

155
00:11:34,980 --> 00:11:41,720
And so on board there's an inclinometer and a rate gyro and other sensors as inputs to the analog computer that fly the boat.

156
00:11:42,220 --> 00:11:44,508
The pilot can only run the throttle and the steering wheel.

157
00:11:44,509 --> 00:11:46,060
Everything else is done by the computer.

158
00:11:47,060 --> 00:11:49,920
When his cabin cruiser is running in normal mode,

159
00:11:50,760 --> 00:11:53,000
Harry Larson looks just like another boat owner.

160
00:11:53,900 --> 00:11:58,300
But when he kicks into hydrofoil gear, heads turn across the lake.

161
00:11:59,820 --> 00:12:01,900
Hey, we're at idle, 1,000 RPM.

162
00:12:02,300 --> 00:12:04,560
We're accelerating now to take off.

163
00:12:05,300 --> 00:12:07,520
You'll notice in a moment here the bow will lift up.

164
00:12:07,820 --> 00:12:09,340
The front foil is lifting the boat,

165
00:12:09,860 --> 00:12:12,620
and we'll accelerate forward by pushing the throttle forward.

166
00:12:16,060 --> 00:12:19,780
And we're flying.

167
00:12:22,340 --> 00:12:28,120
If you haven't guessed, Harry Larson has a lot of fun flying his homemade hydrofoil.

168
00:12:29,100 --> 00:12:34,480
It may not be Ahab from XXX, but he may be getting a call if there's a sequel.

169
00:12:36,860 --> 00:12:43,180
The father of the hydrofoil boat was none other than Alexander Graham Bell, the inventor of the telephone.

170
00:12:43,820 --> 00:12:46,600
Greatest Movie Gadgets will return on Modern Marvels.

171
00:12:52,380 --> 00:12:56,440
What better way to spice up a show about movie guy gadgets

172
00:12:56,441 --> 00:12:58,700
than with another word that begins with G.

173
00:12:59,140 --> 00:12:59,840
Gun.

174
00:13:01,740 --> 00:13:05,080
Ever since the first frame of film was shot for the movies,

175
00:13:05,340 --> 00:13:07,660
guns were shot as part of the plot line.

176
00:13:08,100 --> 00:13:10,820
Desperate, dangerous men, hooded and masked,

177
00:13:11,320 --> 00:13:12,160
ravaged the land.

178
00:13:12,161 --> 00:13:17,160
robbing, killing, setting fire to homes and farms.

179
00:13:17,161 --> 00:13:21,160
Western gunslingers had their favorites, mainly Colt .45s.

180
00:13:21,161 --> 00:13:25,160
We gotta hold up.

181
00:13:29,160 --> 00:13:33,160
Gangsters did most of their business with Tommy guns.

182
00:13:34,160 --> 00:13:38,160
Soldiers in war movies used every gun imaginable,

183
00:13:38,161 --> 00:13:41,160
from sidearms to rocket launchers.

184
00:13:42,160 --> 00:13:50,960
When a gang of foreign agents plot to kill the leading scientists of the world, Jeff King,

185
00:13:50,961 --> 00:13:54,900
Thunderbolt of the Air, wrecks their plans by his heroic action.

186
00:13:55,240 --> 00:13:59,840
And sci-fi movies? Imaginative prop designers had a field day here.

187
00:14:04,060 --> 00:14:07,060
From the disintegrator of movie serial days...

188
00:14:07,061 --> 00:14:11,080
Wait, when it is completed, it will be capable of destroying the universe.

189
00:14:11,081 --> 00:14:14,280
to any form of space-age weapon imaginable.

190
00:14:18,020 --> 00:14:19,300
Run for that crevice.

191
00:14:24,760 --> 00:14:26,600
Which brings us to today.

192
00:14:28,040 --> 00:14:33,760
Now, there's a big-screen weapon that has set a new standard as the gun of all gods.

193
00:14:34,300 --> 00:14:36,940
It was designed for the movie The Fifth Element.

194
00:14:37,740 --> 00:14:39,360
Voila, the GF-1.

195
00:14:41,080 --> 00:14:43,080
Slide.

196
00:14:43,081 --> 00:14:45,080
Handle's adjustable for easy carrying.

197
00:14:45,081 --> 00:14:46,080
Good for right as and left.

198
00:14:46,081 --> 00:14:47,080
It strikes down on four parts.

199
00:14:47,081 --> 00:14:48,080
Undetectable by x-ray.

200
00:14:48,081 --> 00:14:50,080
Ideal for quick discrete interventions.

201
00:14:50,081 --> 00:14:52,080
One shot.

202
00:14:52,081 --> 00:14:56,080
And replay sends every following shot to the same location.

203
00:15:03,080 --> 00:15:05,080
And to finish the job,

204
00:15:05,081 --> 00:15:07,080
all those all goldies but goldies.

205
00:15:07,081 --> 00:15:09,080
Rocket launcher.

206
00:15:11,080 --> 00:15:14,200
Arrow launcher with explosion of poisonous gas head

207
00:15:16,600 --> 00:15:18,600
Our famous net launcher

208
00:15:19,560 --> 00:15:21,560
The always efficient flamethrower

209
00:15:25,800 --> 00:15:27,800
My favorite

210
00:15:27,801 --> 00:15:31,640
And for the grand finale, the all-new Ice Cube system

211
00:15:31,641 --> 00:15:45,641
From the get-go, writer-director Luc Besson wanted the ZF-1 to be the gun against which all future motion picture weapons would be measured.

212
00:15:49,680 --> 00:15:54,760
British movie gun guru Simon Atherton was charged with delivering the goods.

213
00:15:54,761 --> 00:16:00,040
Luke was quite specific in what he wanted the gun to do

214
00:16:00,041 --> 00:16:03,840
and with his concept drawing he said that he wanted a net to come out of it

215
00:16:03,841 --> 00:16:05,960
he didn't say where the net had to come out

216
00:16:05,961 --> 00:16:09,760
he wanted a rocket, he wanted it to be a flamethrower

217
00:16:09,761 --> 00:16:12,380
he wanted his ice effect to happen

218
00:16:12,381 --> 00:16:18,960
and I think it was left up to us to pick which nozzle would do the appropriate job

219
00:16:18,961 --> 00:16:21,000
It's hard to imagine

220
00:16:21,001 --> 00:16:26,900
But only 20% of what this mother-of-all-weapons does on screen came from computers.

221
00:16:27,880 --> 00:16:32,460
The gun fired 9mm blanks and a real 8-foot square net.

222
00:16:32,840 --> 00:16:34,640
The rocket launcher shot fireworks.

223
00:16:35,440 --> 00:16:38,820
The freeze effect came from an off-camera CO2 tank.

224
00:16:39,320 --> 00:16:44,140
And the arrows? The actual prop gun shot them as well with a pull of the trigger.

225
00:16:45,140 --> 00:16:49,240
That, as a guy like Rambo would say, is one hell of a gun.

226
00:16:49,241 --> 00:16:55,200
But could a weapon like the ZF-1 be made to work on real-world battlefields?

227
00:16:56,620 --> 00:17:05,900
I don't think, you know, your individual soldier will be running around with a flamethrower or a freezing attachment on his gun.

228
00:17:06,260 --> 00:17:10,720
But multifunction weapons, I think, are a thing that's coming into use now.

229
00:17:11,380 --> 00:17:12,180
You'll see more of them.

230
00:17:13,380 --> 00:17:16,480
What Simon says is the absolute truth.

231
00:17:16,481 --> 00:17:20,780
gun enthusiasts meet the XM29

232
00:17:20,781 --> 00:17:24,600
the most incredible, versatile, powerful weapon

233
00:17:24,601 --> 00:17:26,560
ever designed for military use

234
00:17:26,561 --> 00:17:29,940
right now it's in the testing stage

235
00:17:29,941 --> 00:17:32,960
but by 2008

236
00:17:32,961 --> 00:17:37,100
the US Army plans for it to take the place of the gun

237
00:17:37,101 --> 00:17:40,140
that's been a soldier's best friend for nearly 40 years

238
00:17:40,141 --> 00:17:42,360
the M16

239
00:17:42,361 --> 00:17:52,580
The XM29 may not be able to freeze anybody or cast a net, but what it can do is just plain awesome.

240
00:17:53,920 --> 00:17:58,460
For all practical purposes, it's a computer that shoots.

241
00:18:01,140 --> 00:18:07,940
Bernd Hardenstein is a program manager at Heckler & Koch, the German company designing the weapon.

242
00:18:07,941 --> 00:18:12,860
The XM-29 is a weapon which is actually two weapons in one.

243
00:18:12,861 --> 00:18:15,960
The two weapons modules are located over and under.

244
00:18:16,480 --> 00:18:20,380
Underneath we've got a 5.56 assault rifle.

245
00:18:21,100 --> 00:18:25,460
And on top we've got a 20mm grenade launcher.

246
00:18:26,820 --> 00:18:30,240
Nothing about the XM-29 is normal.

247
00:18:31,120 --> 00:18:35,820
Take, for example, the electronic sights used to aim the assault ammo firing mechanism.

248
00:18:35,821 --> 00:18:45,920
Invisible to anyone but the shooter, a laser beam focuses on a target, then bounces back to the XM-29's fire control system.

249
00:18:46,920 --> 00:18:51,620
A computer automatically calculates the range, bearing, and angle to target.

250
00:18:52,460 --> 00:18:57,360
Looking through the eyepiece, the soldier sees a red dot, or aim point.

251
00:18:58,220 --> 00:19:02,980
A pull of the trigger takes out whatever or whomever the dot is focused upon.

252
00:19:05,820 --> 00:19:18,960
But while the aiming mechanism is impressive, the most significant high-tech advance of the XM-29 involves its ability to launch intelligent grenades.

253
00:19:20,300 --> 00:19:25,398
We have to tell the grenade where and when to burst.

254
00:19:25,399 --> 00:19:29,320
That is done with a fire control system.

255
00:19:29,321 --> 00:19:35,560
This weapon gives us the feature to directly fire onto a target that you actually don't see,

256
00:19:35,700 --> 00:19:38,160
a target which is hidden behind a rock.

257
00:19:39,720 --> 00:19:41,140
How is this possible?

258
00:19:41,800 --> 00:19:45,140
When a soldier aims his XM-29 to fire a grenade,

259
00:19:45,940 --> 00:19:50,220
an electronic viewfinder automatically calculates the distance to the target.

260
00:19:51,240 --> 00:19:53,580
Normal ammunition would explode upon impact.

261
00:19:53,581 --> 00:20:02,300
But with the XM-29, the operator uses a control device beside the trigger to set what amounts to an electronic fuse.

262
00:20:03,460 --> 00:20:10,120
Each press of a button programs the grenade to explode one meter closer or farther than the target.

263
00:20:10,780 --> 00:20:11,595
The result?

264
00:20:11,596 --> 00:20:20,340
The ability of the ammunition to penetrate a window or wall and wait to explode until it reaches the center of a room.

265
00:20:23,580 --> 00:20:31,760
This thing can shoot targets no other weapon system in the world today can shoot.

266
00:20:32,360 --> 00:20:35,940
In foxholes, behind walls, inside of rooms.

267
00:20:36,540 --> 00:20:38,500
This is the only gun that can do that.

268
00:20:39,200 --> 00:20:43,700
What makes this gun great to me, though, is that it saved soldiers' lives on the battlefield.

269
00:20:46,540 --> 00:20:51,120
Because the XM-29 relies on sophisticated computer technology,

270
00:20:51,121 --> 00:20:55,120
That means it also needs electricity.

271
00:20:55,121 --> 00:20:59,120
The batteries powering the weapon are state-of-the-art.

272
00:20:59,121 --> 00:21:03,120
But what happens if the power fails?

273
00:21:03,121 --> 00:21:07,120
You still do have a weapon that can fire both rounds.

274
00:21:07,121 --> 00:21:12,120
It can fire the 5.56 round, and it can fire the 20mm round.

275
00:21:12,121 --> 00:21:15,120
You are no longer able to program the round,

276
00:21:15,121 --> 00:21:18,120
but you still have a grenade that's going downrange,

277
00:21:18,121 --> 00:21:23,020
and if it's not programmed, it always goes PD, meaning point detonating.

278
00:21:23,140 --> 00:21:25,080
If it hits a target, it goes.

279
00:21:26,640 --> 00:21:32,360
The XM-29 will be to infantry weapons what the Apache has become to military helicopters

280
00:21:32,361 --> 00:21:37,000
and the stealth to bombers.

281
00:21:39,080 --> 00:21:44,080
Our modeling and simulation has given us that this weapon system may be as much as five times more lethal

282
00:21:44,081 --> 00:21:45,960
than the standard weapon in the arms of soldiers.

283
00:21:45,961 --> 00:21:48,720
that means more soldiers come home alive

284
00:21:48,721 --> 00:21:52,340
just as the ZF-1

285
00:21:52,341 --> 00:21:54,160
is the weapon to beat in the movies

286
00:21:54,161 --> 00:21:56,280
the XM-29 should be

287
00:21:56,281 --> 00:21:58,420
the gun of guns in the real world

288
00:21:58,421 --> 00:22:00,040
for a long time to come

289
00:22:00,041 --> 00:22:04,340
the sleeve gun used in the 1960s

290
00:22:04,341 --> 00:22:06,300
television series the Wild Wild West

291
00:22:06,301 --> 00:22:08,520
is actually a variation of the glove pistol

292
00:22:08,521 --> 00:22:10,760
produced by the Office of Strategic Services

293
00:22:10,761 --> 00:22:11,940
during World War II

294
00:22:11,941 --> 00:22:14,320
Modern Marvels will return in a moment

295
00:22:15,960 --> 00:22:23,600
Film director James Cameron has always been known for his fascination with the mysteries of the sea.

296
00:22:25,040 --> 00:22:31,000
Titanic brought him the most acclaim, but an earlier film titled The Abyss was also spectacular,

297
00:22:31,580 --> 00:22:33,700
especially for its underwater effects.

298
00:22:35,620 --> 00:22:38,840
Explorer, this is Cab 3, starting our descent along the umbilical.

299
00:22:39,980 --> 00:22:41,640
Roger that, Cab 3. Good luck.

300
00:22:41,800 --> 00:22:43,020
Luck is not a factor.

301
00:22:43,021 --> 00:22:49,360
One of the high-tech stars was an undersea utility vehicle called Flatbed,

302
00:22:49,820 --> 00:22:53,520
a challenge for production designers since they first learned of Cameron's vision.

303
00:22:56,420 --> 00:23:00,540
It wouldn't have been so difficult if the vessel could be filmed above water

304
00:23:00,541 --> 00:23:04,260
and made to look like it was under the sea via special effects.

305
00:23:05,260 --> 00:23:07,600
But Cameron wanted the real thing.

306
00:23:07,601 --> 00:23:13,760
It was clear right from the start that Jim wanted to do all this truly underwater.

307
00:23:14,280 --> 00:23:24,200
So it was a matter of safety and practicality and cost to incorporate some already well-engineered underwater technology into the submarine.

308
00:23:25,120 --> 00:23:29,280
Ron Cobb knew how to design props that looked cool underwater.

309
00:23:29,700 --> 00:23:33,100
He did not know how to build them so they could actually sustain life.

310
00:23:33,940 --> 00:23:39,300
So he searched and searched for a sub he could turn into flatbed.

311
00:23:39,680 --> 00:23:42,100
He found it in Vancouver, British Columbia.

312
00:23:43,480 --> 00:23:48,760
The prominent front-end glass sphere from which pilots guided the fictional movie vessel

313
00:23:48,761 --> 00:23:52,240
was an honest-to-goodness real deep-sea vessel

314
00:23:52,241 --> 00:23:57,160
invented by Phil Newton, an internationally recognized oceanic explorer.

315
00:23:57,161 --> 00:24:00,280
The real sphere is called Deep Rover.

316
00:24:03,100 --> 00:24:09,300
I'm often asked whether Cameron got the idea from our sub or whether we got the idea from his sub in the movie.

317
00:24:09,740 --> 00:24:12,720
In fact, we'd already been operating this sub for well over 10 years.

318
00:24:12,920 --> 00:24:15,000
Primarily, it's a research and working tool.

319
00:24:15,001 --> 00:24:20,340
This submarine dives to 3,000 feet, primarily for science, for environmental research,

320
00:24:20,640 --> 00:24:26,660
but a lot of things like salvage and cable inspections and all the sorts of mundane commercial work that we normally do.

321
00:24:28,620 --> 00:24:32,000
Deep Rover is a masterpiece of deep-sea technology.

322
00:24:33,100 --> 00:24:39,600
One of the biggest challenges for its designers was figuring out a way to filter air which the operator could breathe.

323
00:24:40,220 --> 00:24:42,980
The solution came in the form of scrubbers.

324
00:24:44,300 --> 00:24:50,920
There's one scrubber canister here, and this converts the CO2, the carbon dioxide, that you breathe out.

325
00:24:50,921 --> 00:24:58,920
It absorbs it, and just like the backpack on a space suit or an astronaut, this will absorb the CO2.

326
00:24:58,921 --> 00:25:03,320
There's an oxygen control system one on each side just like there's a scrubber on each side

327
00:25:03,321 --> 00:25:08,300
So are two completely redundant system now the life support in this sub is over 80 hours

328
00:25:08,800 --> 00:25:10,860
So that's more than three days

329
00:25:11,020 --> 00:25:15,680
So even if one complete system went out you still have a day and a half of life support

330
00:25:15,681 --> 00:25:20,100
And by that time you should be able to figure out a how to get up or be how to be rescued

331
00:25:20,101 --> 00:25:24,700
It looks a very complex system once you know how to operate it

332
00:25:24,701 --> 00:25:27,480
Heck, it's just like driving a golf cart. Nothing to it.

333
00:25:27,740 --> 00:25:31,100
And you have all the time in the world to marvel at what you're seeing.

334
00:25:31,480 --> 00:25:34,020
But when you look out of this particular windshield,

335
00:25:34,620 --> 00:25:37,480
you're seeing things that no one in the whole world has ever seen before.

336
00:25:38,640 --> 00:25:39,860
Cutting you loose, bud.

337
00:25:40,080 --> 00:25:42,900
Okay, one night. Stay clear. We're sitting her down.

338
00:25:43,060 --> 00:25:47,900
In the abyss, Deep Rover got a vacation from its day job and became a star.

339
00:25:50,840 --> 00:25:51,540
Touchdown!

340
00:25:51,541 --> 00:25:53,100
But not without a major makeover.

341
00:25:53,900 --> 00:25:56,580
Based on the directorial vision of James Cameron,

342
00:25:57,260 --> 00:26:01,420
production designer Ron Cobb needed to turn this into this,

343
00:26:01,760 --> 00:26:04,940
and the entire unit needed to function underwater.

344
00:26:05,800 --> 00:26:08,220
This was one of the earlier black and white sketches.

345
00:26:08,460 --> 00:26:11,200
I was trying to hone in on the actual submarine configuration,

346
00:26:11,201 --> 00:26:15,320
but it actually came down to something like this,

347
00:26:15,420 --> 00:26:19,880
which was a fairly simple shape with the conning tower to the rear.

348
00:26:19,881 --> 00:26:25,700
the thruster housings very large with the life support sphere in the front

349
00:26:25,701 --> 00:26:27,400
with all these little attitude thrusters.

350
00:26:27,600 --> 00:26:29,640
And it turned out pretty much like this,

351
00:26:29,720 --> 00:26:33,100
except that we did have to move the conning tower forward for safety reasons.

352
00:26:33,260 --> 00:26:36,240
But that's pretty much the final drawing on it.

353
00:26:37,280 --> 00:26:39,960
Once Ron Cobb had completed the design,

354
00:26:40,460 --> 00:26:43,920
it was up to undersea mock-up builder Don Pennington

355
00:26:43,921 --> 00:26:47,420
to physically make Deep Rover a part of Flatbed.

356
00:26:47,421 --> 00:26:54,400
When I got to Phil's business in Canada, it was, well, this is real.

357
00:26:54,840 --> 00:26:56,700
We're really going to be putting real people in here.

358
00:26:57,060 --> 00:26:58,400
This is going to be a great project.

359
00:26:58,880 --> 00:27:04,540
And how am I going to marry Ron Cobb's drawings to these submarines that are already built?

360
00:27:05,980 --> 00:27:10,840
Pennington had eight weeks to build flatbed so that it fit perfectly with Deep Rover.

361
00:27:10,841 --> 00:27:19,520
Some of the challenges that we had to consider were that all the pieces that we made had to be attached to his sub,

362
00:27:19,620 --> 00:27:23,100
which would not interfere with his propulsion and life support systems.

363
00:27:23,900 --> 00:27:27,960
Everything that we built had to work underwater, not dissolve, come unglued.

364
00:27:28,240 --> 00:27:29,540
It was a very busy script.

365
00:27:30,040 --> 00:27:33,660
All hell must be breaking loose up there. This cable's pissing me off.

366
00:27:34,920 --> 00:27:36,840
I can't get a grip on it.

367
00:27:36,841 --> 00:27:43,840
People are walking on it, they're putting oil drilling pipes on it, there's fights on it.

368
00:27:47,840 --> 00:27:54,840
There's a mechanical arm that we built a mock-up for because the real arm couldn't be left on all the time.

369
00:28:06,840 --> 00:28:12,120
The real arms of Deep Rover could not be used constantly during filming,

370
00:28:12,580 --> 00:28:15,520
for fear of damaging the incredibly complex machines.

371
00:28:17,040 --> 00:28:22,440
These arms and this submersible, I mean, they are an amazing piece of technology, no question about it.

372
00:28:22,840 --> 00:28:26,500
This little guy right here, this little electrical fitting, which looks like nothing,

373
00:28:26,880 --> 00:28:28,980
this is a sensory feedback system.

374
00:28:29,320 --> 00:28:32,660
And what happens is the jaws and the arm emit a hum.

375
00:28:33,000 --> 00:28:35,760
So as you're working with these things, you press on something,

376
00:28:35,761 --> 00:28:47,160
If you come towards something, you hear, mmm, as you press on it, mmm, mmm, and you can tell exactly how much pressure you're putting on something.

377
00:28:47,200 --> 00:28:53,080
You could pick up an egg because the jaws are humming to you as you pick it up, and the slightest bit of pressure changes the pitch.

378
00:28:53,480 --> 00:28:57,840
And even though you can't see it and you can't sense it, you can hear it so clearly.

379
00:28:57,841 --> 00:29:05,260
You can actually rub the jaws across various materials, wood, concrete, glass, and feel the difference in texture.

380
00:29:05,760 --> 00:29:12,440
When the day arrived for Flatbed and Deep Rover to work as one, both Pennington and Newton were ready.

381
00:29:13,360 --> 00:29:18,160
Filming took place in a 30-foot deep tank filled with 5 million gallons of water.

382
00:29:19,100 --> 00:29:23,880
Pennington held his breath when the combined unit was first lowered into the abyss.

383
00:29:25,780 --> 00:29:33,700
Because we had taken the initial design and modified it so much down there where it had to do more and more and more,

384
00:29:33,701 --> 00:29:43,840
those first few times are always the god don't break don't split apart to james cameron's delight

385
00:29:43,841 --> 00:29:51,360
and don pennington's relief flatbed worked like a charm pennington couldn't resist taking a ride

386
00:29:51,361 --> 00:29:57,360
in the deep rover sphere as you're being lowered into the water it's like yes i'm going in it's a

387
00:29:57,361 --> 00:30:02,560
real sub you know i could be down there breathing and and you go in and they start motoring the sub

388
00:30:02,561 --> 00:30:07,940
around it's just really exciting and then of course the divers all come over and they say hi

389
00:30:07,941 --> 00:30:13,460
and make rude signs but it was just it was really an exciting feeling and just that you know it's

390
00:30:13,461 --> 00:30:18,680
the feeling has got to be the same 30 feet 20 feet underwater as it does at a thousand feet

391
00:30:18,681 --> 00:30:25,860
no one appreciates that exhilarating feeling more than phil newton when you're in the water

392
00:30:25,861 --> 00:30:28,040
the plastic disappears

393
00:30:28,041 --> 00:30:31,080
and it feels like you're sitting in this framework

394
00:30:31,081 --> 00:30:34,440
in this seat inside of a bubble in the ocean.

395
00:30:35,160 --> 00:30:36,620
And when I first dove the sub

396
00:30:36,621 --> 00:30:39,160
I'd been used to diving conventional type subs

397
00:30:39,161 --> 00:30:41,360
and the first thing when the water closed over my head

398
00:30:41,361 --> 00:30:42,061
and I looked around

399
00:30:42,062 --> 00:30:43,680
I kind of gulped a couple of times

400
00:30:43,681 --> 00:30:46,560
and I thought, geez, there's a lot of water out here

401
00:30:46,561 --> 00:30:48,900
and to see all around you

402
00:30:48,901 --> 00:30:50,540
and to see behind you and below you

403
00:30:50,541 --> 00:30:52,040
it's kind of like flying a helicopter.

404
00:30:52,520 --> 00:30:54,760
You can look straight down below you

405
00:30:54,761 --> 00:30:59,660
You can look up, you can look forward and to the sides, and just a great sub to drive.

406
00:30:59,940 --> 00:31:01,880
It's sometimes been called an underwater sports car.

407
00:31:02,840 --> 00:31:03,860
Flatbed, you online?

408
00:31:04,640 --> 00:31:06,500
10-4 Lindsay, we read you loud and clear.

409
00:31:09,300 --> 00:31:11,540
Starting our descent. Divers, how you doing?

410
00:31:12,400 --> 00:31:13,760
Everybody's okay so far.

411
00:31:14,480 --> 00:31:17,020
As anyone who has seen the abyss will attest,

412
00:31:17,720 --> 00:31:23,580
the combined efforts of the masters of movie magic and real undersea exploration looked good.

413
00:31:24,760 --> 00:31:26,400
Very, very good.

414
00:31:28,860 --> 00:31:30,620
Cap 3, it's Bud. You online?

415
00:31:31,640 --> 00:31:33,860
Cap 3, check. I'm right behind you.

416
00:31:38,240 --> 00:31:39,820
Cap 1, do you see it yet?

417
00:31:39,821 --> 00:31:44,200
For Don Pennington, the only sad moment occurred when the filming ended.

418
00:31:45,260 --> 00:31:48,060
It was kind of disheartening because when they wrapped the subs,

419
00:31:48,180 --> 00:31:51,420
they were in a million pieces because of the fight sequence.

420
00:31:51,520 --> 00:31:54,320
They just completely destroyed the subs.

421
00:31:54,321 --> 00:31:56,300
But it was a good feeling.

422
00:31:56,500 --> 00:31:58,820
It did everything that Jim wanted it to do.

423
00:31:59,460 --> 00:32:02,540
The subs gave real good production value for the show.

424
00:32:03,420 --> 00:32:07,560
And what does the future hold for even more exciting undersea adventures,

425
00:32:08,060 --> 00:32:09,980
both in the movies and the real world?

426
00:32:11,000 --> 00:32:14,380
How about a submarine that's actually a suit?

427
00:32:16,420 --> 00:32:22,100
Undersea pioneer Phil Newton broke new ground in the early 1980s with his newt suit,

428
00:32:22,101 --> 00:32:26,440
allowing divers to function with patented, rotatable joints.

429
00:32:27,220 --> 00:32:29,900
Now, he's preparing the next generation.

430
00:32:31,360 --> 00:32:33,920
What these are called are exosuits.

431
00:32:34,360 --> 00:32:38,200
An exo from exoskeleton, like the hard shell on a crab.

432
00:32:38,580 --> 00:32:41,480
And this suit essentially protects you from pressure.

433
00:32:41,920 --> 00:32:45,700
So inside this suit, the diver or the pilot, whatever you want to call them,

434
00:32:46,060 --> 00:32:49,320
is at exactly the same pressure that we're at in this room right now.

435
00:32:49,321 --> 00:32:53,980
So you can dive at depths down to 1,000 feet and feel no effects from pressure.

436
00:32:55,120 --> 00:32:58,640
And if you thought the arms and claws in Deep Rover were amazing,

437
00:32:59,140 --> 00:33:02,060
check out the prototype fingers for the exosuit.

438
00:33:03,040 --> 00:33:07,940
This is the prehensor, and this is the closest you can get to the human hand.

439
00:33:08,140 --> 00:33:09,680
And you see it works directly.

440
00:33:10,040 --> 00:33:14,100
It's called a direct link prehensor because it gives you a prehensal grip

441
00:33:14,101 --> 00:33:17,200
as you see these two digits opposed here, just like your thumbs.

442
00:33:17,201 --> 00:33:23,940
And you see that what's actually happening here is I'm doing the same thing inside the hand pod as is happening outside.

443
00:33:24,760 --> 00:33:30,800
And so this, for the first time ever, gives you the ability to sweep across, to index this thumb wherever you want it,

444
00:33:31,020 --> 00:33:34,400
to grip things and carry it just in your fingertips if you wished.

445
00:33:34,960 --> 00:33:37,600
And this is just a beta model.

446
00:33:37,880 --> 00:33:40,500
If it was any smaller, you'd be on the outside looking in.

447
00:33:40,620 --> 00:33:45,800
So this is as small as a submarine can get, and this truly is a submarine that you wear.

448
00:33:47,200 --> 00:33:56,620
In August 2000, the Central Intelligence Agency, the CIA, held a one-time exhibit which featured 400 famous movie and television spy gadgets.

