00:35:07,240 --> 00:35:14,280
In the animated cartoon series The Jetsons, George Jetson took flying vehicles to a more practical level,

461
00:35:14,281 --> 00:35:18,280
Commuting to and from work in a flying saucer-like car.

462
00:35:20,120 --> 00:35:24,660
It doesn't take much research to understand our fascination with flying cars.

463
00:35:25,480 --> 00:35:29,000
In fact, it only takes an hour riding the brake in rush hour traffic.

464
00:35:31,020 --> 00:35:35,080
Imagine that you're stuck in such a traffic jam and something magical occurs.

465
00:35:35,760 --> 00:35:38,940
Your car is able to lift off the ground like a helicopter.

466
00:35:38,941 --> 00:35:44,460
Then, kick into gear and fly forward at speeds up to 300 miles per hour,

467
00:35:45,000 --> 00:35:48,620
far above the thousands of poor souls cursing you from below.

468
00:35:49,220 --> 00:35:52,040
It sounds pretty far-fetched, but it's not.

469
00:35:53,040 --> 00:35:57,440
Ladies and gentlemen, the world's first real flying car.

470
00:35:58,060 --> 00:36:01,320
And it's designer Paul Moeller of Sacramento, California.

471
00:36:02,660 --> 00:36:04,780
Well, this is a vehicle that we call a Skycar.

472
00:36:04,980 --> 00:36:07,280
It's a combination helicopter and airplane.

473
00:36:07,281 --> 00:36:12,260
It has the capability to take it off vertically and then transitioning to high-speed Ford flights.

474
00:36:12,400 --> 00:36:13,520
So you've got the best of two worlds.

475
00:36:13,920 --> 00:36:18,260
It's also capable of driving on the ground as a legal vehicle on the highway.

476
00:36:19,640 --> 00:36:23,240
Paul Moeller, with a team of 30 electricians and engineers,

477
00:36:23,700 --> 00:36:29,500
has spent 40 years and $200 million of investment money trying to make that dream come true.

478
00:36:29,940 --> 00:36:36,080
His first attempt, looking more like a flying saucer than a car, actually flew 15 years ago.

479
00:36:36,081 --> 00:36:42,380
But the engines were a problem, not light and powerful enough to support sustained flight.

480
00:36:43,500 --> 00:36:48,520
Well, at that time, we had very small engines, which meant that this really couldn't fly more than a couple feet off the ground.

481
00:36:49,360 --> 00:36:55,280
But as time went along and we developed more powerful engines, then we were able to put on more and more impressive flights.

482
00:36:55,740 --> 00:37:03,240
The final flight, 1989, we flew before the international press, a nice two-minute flight where we maneuvered up to about 80 feet,

483
00:37:03,241 --> 00:37:08,960
turned in various directions, satisfied everybody, and came back down and thank God that I was still alive.

484
00:37:10,440 --> 00:37:15,260
Mohler retired his flying saucer and devoted his energy to designing new engines.

485
00:37:16,280 --> 00:37:23,720
He calculated that in order for a flying car to be practical, engines had to deliver two horsepower per pound of weight.

486
00:37:24,500 --> 00:37:26,920
The average automobile delivers less than one.

487
00:37:26,921 --> 00:37:35,960
The eight engines on the new Skycar not only meet the horsepower requirements, but can use a variety of fuels, including alcohol.

488
00:37:37,000 --> 00:37:39,900
The engines are now undergoing extensive testing.

489
00:37:41,840 --> 00:37:44,740
The Skycar is a relatively efficient vehicle.

490
00:37:44,980 --> 00:37:47,840
Of course, not when it's hovering, because of course it's not going anywhere, it's burning lots of fuel.

491
00:37:47,900 --> 00:37:52,280
But as soon as it starts to move, it becomes very efficient, like a good airplane.

492
00:37:53,080 --> 00:37:56,680
We can get as high as 28 miles a gallon at the most efficient flying speed.

493
00:37:56,681 --> 00:38:01,620
Now, people don't always go at the most efficient speeds, so I'd say perhaps most people average 20 miles a gallon.

494
00:38:02,240 --> 00:38:05,520
That's still not bad when you're going over 300 miles an hour at 25,000 feet.

495
00:38:07,540 --> 00:38:11,660
Moeller is particularly proud of the vehicle's simple user interface.

496
00:38:12,440 --> 00:38:19,480
The terms steering wheel or dashboard don't seem appropriate because the Skycar is basically a flying computer.

497
00:38:21,300 --> 00:38:26,420
One of the things you'll notice in the Skycar if you compare with average airplanes is you have very few switches.

498
00:38:26,421 --> 00:38:29,480
The only reason you have even what we have here is because we have eight engines,

499
00:38:29,620 --> 00:38:31,920
so we have on-off switches for the engines.

500
00:38:32,100 --> 00:38:35,120
But otherwise, everything that goes on is seen on the screens.

501
00:38:35,220 --> 00:38:39,080
You have touchscreens here that basically do or tell you everything you need to know.

502
00:38:39,500 --> 00:38:41,800
And as far as flying it, you increase the throttle setting.

503
00:38:41,920 --> 00:38:42,620
That lifts you up.

504
00:38:42,960 --> 00:38:45,500
You move the stick forward, you go forward, or you leave it alone,

505
00:38:45,600 --> 00:38:48,020
and you sit there and you can look around and just sit there and hover

506
00:38:48,021 --> 00:38:49,960
because the machine will take care of itself.

507
00:38:50,060 --> 00:38:53,360
It will keep you absolutely where you want to be during this process.

508
00:38:53,361 --> 00:39:01,200
At this point in time, Mohler has flown the Skycar seven times, but always tethered to

509
00:39:01,201 --> 00:39:05,320
a crane behind the manufacturing plant.

510
00:39:05,321 --> 00:39:11,080
At this stage of development, he can't risk a crash of his sole multi-million dollar prototype

511
00:39:11,081 --> 00:39:16,740
or a jail term for operating an aircraft in an industrial park.

512
00:39:16,741 --> 00:39:21,200
As you can see from the videotapes, the Skycar does indeed fly.

513
00:39:23,060 --> 00:39:28,500
When driven on ground, the car's four front engines remain in a horizontal position.

514
00:39:29,260 --> 00:39:35,160
They rotate vertically for a straight-up take-off, then return to horizontal for airborne acceleration.

515
00:39:36,500 --> 00:39:43,320
Mohler claims the Skycar can take off faster than a mile a minute and accelerate faster than a Ferrari Testarossa,

516
00:39:44,080 --> 00:39:45,800
Which brings up the obvious question.

517
00:39:46,420 --> 00:39:48,320
Will it cost as much as a Ferrari?

518
00:39:49,840 --> 00:39:52,860
Ten years, I think you're still talking over 100,000,

519
00:39:53,300 --> 00:39:56,960
except if the volume really happens faster than I would imagine,

520
00:39:57,120 --> 00:40:00,080
which means if you could produce 500,000 of these vehicles a year,

521
00:40:00,600 --> 00:40:02,920
which is still small in automotive standards,

522
00:40:03,320 --> 00:40:05,540
then it would be down to about 65,000.

523
00:40:07,060 --> 00:40:11,580
And when might we expect to see the first production models roll off an assembly line?

524
00:40:13,320 --> 00:40:18,720
This Skycar right now is really ready to go from the point of view of its mechanics, its electronics.

525
00:40:19,340 --> 00:40:23,520
The thing that's going to keep it from being out there in huge numbers is actually not this vehicle,

526
00:40:23,660 --> 00:40:25,960
but it's the highway in the sky that is coming into place.

527
00:40:26,060 --> 00:40:28,720
And that's probably four years away.

528
00:40:29,200 --> 00:40:32,660
We think the vehicle will be in modest production in that same four-year period,

529
00:40:32,820 --> 00:40:34,600
so perhaps they'll get together around that time.

530
00:40:35,160 --> 00:40:39,280
You won't see large numbers perhaps until ten years from now, but it's close by.

531
00:40:39,281 --> 00:40:43,320
And just what is the highway in the sky?

532
00:40:45,460 --> 00:40:46,240
It's interesting.

533
00:40:46,241 --> 00:40:49,820
A lot of people don't realize that we're in the process of organizing the sky.

534
00:40:50,060 --> 00:40:53,660
The sky has been basically chaotic since the invention of the airplane.

535
00:40:54,200 --> 00:41:00,000
And they have these new programs coming in a place where they use geostationary satellites and GPS.

536
00:41:00,380 --> 00:41:04,400
And together with this, you're able to know where every vehicle is within a few feet all the time.

537
00:41:04,401 --> 00:41:10,400
And therefore you can set up a program that gets everybody on a specific virtual highway in the sky.

538
00:41:10,620 --> 00:41:12,700
It's not a complicated process to put together.

539
00:41:12,840 --> 00:41:17,280
It's not that expensive compared to throwing down concrete right-of-ways and pothole filling.

540
00:41:17,840 --> 00:41:21,340
All we really need to do is change our national priorities a little bit

541
00:41:21,341 --> 00:41:24,720
and recognize that traffic's a major problem today, and this is a solution.

542
00:41:26,080 --> 00:41:30,440
According to Mohler, representatives of several major auto manufacturers

543
00:41:30,441 --> 00:41:32,940
have visited his plant to check out the Skycar.

544
00:41:33,520 --> 00:41:35,440
But they have declined to invest.

545
00:41:37,260 --> 00:41:39,940
Is there really a flying car in our future?

546
00:41:40,660 --> 00:41:46,080
Or will we have to be content to let our imaginations fly in the comfort of theater seats?

547
00:41:47,620 --> 00:41:56,700
For me, there is no issue about it, because I can see every day the economics of it, the practicality, the safety, so many of the features that this vehicle overcomes.

548
00:41:57,060 --> 00:42:00,600
So I hearken back to the engines as the key to making this whole thing work.

549
00:42:01,060 --> 00:42:02,960
When the engines are there, the Skycar's there.

550
00:42:03,140 --> 00:42:05,260
When the highways are there, you're in the Skycar.

551
00:42:06,660 --> 00:42:12,360
The technological gadgetry that made the Aston Martin car in the Bond movie Die Another Day disappear

552
00:42:12,361 --> 00:42:16,765
was inspired by real-life technology currently under development by the U.S.

553
00:42:16,766 --> 00:42:18,040
Department of Defense.

554
00:42:18,660 --> 00:42:20,940
Greatest Movie Gadgets will return in a moment.

555
00:42:20,941 --> 00:42:34,941
For moviegoers of all ages, Steven Spielberg's Minority Report was a futuristic feast,

556
00:42:36,740 --> 00:42:39,640
imagineered by America's top creative minds.

557
00:42:40,720 --> 00:42:47,820
We brought together about 15 people from around the United States, writers, artists, social thinkers, technologists, and so on.

558
00:42:47,821 --> 00:42:57,058
We brought a demographer from Washington, the head of the MIT Architecture School, a guy from the Media Lab, Doug Copeland, the novelist, the inventor of virtual

559
00:42:57,059 --> 00:42:58,320
reality, Jaron Lanier.

560
00:42:58,321 --> 00:43:01,078
And we assembled all those people in a hotel in L.A.

561
00:43:01,079 --> 00:43:05,640
for several days with Spielberg, the script writers, the producers, the art directors.

562
00:43:05,860 --> 00:43:14,603
And we even had artists and digital artists working on screens around us as we talked, inventing cars, inventing buildings, redesigning Washington, and so on as we did

563
00:43:14,604 --> 00:43:15,304
it.

564
00:43:15,305 --> 00:43:21,760
No scene had more cool factor than the maglev automated highway.

565
00:43:21,761 --> 00:43:28,760
People pods transported passengers without a single touch of a steering wheel, accelerator, or brake.

566
00:43:28,761 --> 00:43:32,760
It first of all began with a request by Steven Spielberg.

567
00:43:32,761 --> 00:43:34,760
We were going to do Washington a half century in the future.

568
00:43:34,761 --> 00:43:36,760
He said, I don't want any traffic jams in my future.

569
00:43:36,761 --> 00:43:41,760
So, you know, we were trying to build a more benign future, a clean, green future, and said no traffic jams.

570
00:43:41,761 --> 00:43:44,691
traffic jams. So this was our solution for traffic jams.

571
00:43:44,692 --> 00:43:46,680
That is a very advanced transit system

572
00:43:46,681 --> 00:43:50,840
that combines the advantages of the automobile with, in fact, mass transit.

573
00:43:51,740 --> 00:43:53,378
How does maglev work?

574
00:43:53,379 --> 00:43:58,840
Well, the name maglev, it did have to do with magnetic and levitation.

575
00:43:58,841 --> 00:44:06,040
It's a magnetically steered system, so the car knows where to go based on devices inside the

576
00:44:06,041 --> 00:44:10,131
cart following probably embedded items in the road.

577
00:44:10,132 --> 00:44:13,260
But maglev also means there's maybe not

578
00:44:13,261 --> 00:44:19,317
exact contact with the ground. The pods aren't really touching the system itself.

579
00:44:19,318 --> 00:44:20,140
Maglev is a

580
00:44:20,141 --> 00:44:21,245
driver's dream.

581
00:44:21,246 --> 00:44:27,360
Sit back, relax, have a cup of coffee, read the paper, while your car automatically

582
00:44:27,361 --> 00:44:29,557
takes you where you need to go.

583
00:44:29,558 --> 00:44:33,880
Visual effects art director Alex Laurent took maglev from its

584
00:44:33,881 --> 00:44:38,080
first black and white sketches through its final production.

585
00:44:38,081 --> 00:44:39,760
But he had one advantage

586
00:44:39,761 --> 00:44:42,405
any real world futurist doesn't have.

587
00:44:42,406 --> 00:44:45,980
The maglev system in Minority Report didn't really

588
00:44:45,981 --> 00:44:49,820
need to work. It only needed to look like it could.

589
00:44:51,380 --> 00:44:54,731
We tried to keep all of this extremely physically real seeming.

590
00:44:54,732 --> 00:44:56,540
At no time did we have to consider

591
00:44:56,541 --> 00:44:57,868
actually manufacturing this.

592
00:44:57,869 --> 00:45:00,760
We didn't have to consider the real load bearing capabilities

593
00:45:00,761 --> 00:45:04,780
of any of these structures, but we had to make them feel that they would work.

594
00:45:05,060 --> 00:45:08,940
And then the ever-mysterious high-tech maglev surface on top.

595
00:45:09,180 --> 00:45:13,080
And at no time do we actually see one long stretch of this stuff edge on,

596
00:45:13,200 --> 00:45:17,100
so we always had the benefit of curvature to create a more pleasing overall effect.

597
00:45:18,680 --> 00:45:23,200
Incredibly, automotive futurists have been experimenting with real maglev systems

598
00:45:23,201 --> 00:45:25,740
in Germany and Japan for several years.

599
00:45:25,741 --> 00:45:33,500
a system is actually being installed between shanghai airport and the city of shanghai

600
00:45:33,501 --> 00:45:40,500
it isn't so much that the technology itself is so expensive i wouldn't say it's much more

601
00:45:40,501 --> 00:45:44,940
expensive than an ordinary vehicle it is the energy cost associated with producing enough

602
00:45:44,941 --> 00:45:50,740
energy electricity in particular to keep something floating that's the issue you're having to

603
00:45:50,741 --> 00:45:54,260
overcome its weight all the time and you don't have wheels to hold it on to support it on the

604
00:45:54,261 --> 00:45:54,961
ground.

605
00:45:54,962 --> 00:45:58,460
So it's just using a lot of energy, and that's really the costly element of it.

606
00:45:59,220 --> 00:46:04,580
Here in the United States, maglev is limited to the silver screen for the time being.

607
00:46:05,420 --> 00:46:10,200
But that doesn't mean other automated highway systems are not being seriously considered.

608
00:46:11,700 --> 00:46:14,076
Several large-scale experiments are underway.

609
00:46:14,077 --> 00:46:16,240
There's one not far from here in Northern

610
00:46:16,241 --> 00:46:19,593
California. There's one developing in Southern California.

611
00:46:19,594 --> 00:46:21,500
The idea being to control cars by

612
00:46:21,501 --> 00:46:26,280
computers so you can get more cars on the highway closer together and minimize the likelihood of

613
00:46:26,281 --> 00:46:26,981
accidents.

614
00:46:26,982 --> 00:46:30,720
This is a hard computer problem, but in fact we are beginning to move in that direction

615
00:46:30,721 --> 00:46:31,675
right now.

616
00:46:31,676 --> 00:46:39,700
On this seven-mile stretch of freeway north of San Diego, California, the middle two

617
00:46:39,701 --> 00:46:44,780
lanes look pretty normal during the weekdays, jam-packed with commuters wishing they lived in

618
00:46:44,781 --> 00:46:45,481
Kansas.

619
00:46:45,482 --> 00:46:52,580
But in the evenings or anytime on weekends, you might catch this amazing sight.

620
00:46:53,440 --> 00:46:59,880
Seven Buicks flying along at 60 miles an hour, just a few feet apart, with their drivers reading,

621
00:47:00,480 --> 00:47:03,123
sleeping, or simply enjoying the ride.

622
00:47:03,124 --> 00:47:06,740
It's a test of an automated highway system different

623
00:47:06,741 --> 00:47:12,700
from MagLab, one that many believe may be financially and logistically feasible in the

624
00:47:12,701 --> 00:47:19,780
United States. There's definitely a mag or magnet involved in the process. Just no

625
00:47:19,781 --> 00:47:21,529
lev or levitation.

626
00:47:21,530 --> 00:47:28,620
Stephen Schladover is director of PATH, Partners for Advanced Transit and

627
00:47:28,621 --> 00:47:34,640
Highways, a research program involving the University of California and the California

628
00:47:34,641 --> 00:47:40,720
Department of Transportation. No one knows the need for rush hour relief better than

629
00:47:40,721 --> 00:47:46,000
California. And this relatively simple configuration of magnets embedded in

630
00:47:46,001 --> 00:47:52,720
highways and computer sensors installed in cars just may be a solution.

631
00:47:52,721 --> 00:47:57,160
We can provide guidance for vehicles by installing very simple and cheap

632
00:47:57,161 --> 00:48:00,840
magnets in the roadway surface. This is an example of the magnets that are

633
00:48:00,841 --> 00:48:05,260
installed in the highway for road guidance right now. Each of these stacks

634
00:48:05,261 --> 00:48:09,940
of magnets cost less than a dollar and we can install them every four feet down

635
00:48:09,941 --> 00:48:13,480
on the center of a lane, or probably even a longer distance than that,

636
00:48:13,560 --> 00:48:15,920
and still be sufficient for vehicle guidance.

637
00:48:16,300 --> 00:48:19,900
So the cost of equipping a lane of highway is really not very high,

638
00:48:20,100 --> 00:48:23,300
and it's a very small fraction of the cost of constructing the highway.

639
00:48:24,260 --> 00:48:28,140
And how would a car need to be equipped to follow the magnets?

640
00:48:29,520 --> 00:48:32,800
This is one of the test vehicles that we've been doing experiments on

641
00:48:32,801 --> 00:48:34,360
for the past six or seven years.

642
00:48:34,600 --> 00:48:37,960
It has magnetic sensors mounted in front of the bumper.

643
00:48:37,961 --> 00:48:39,785
You can see there are three sensors.

644
00:48:39,786 --> 00:48:43,080
Those measure the magnetic fields of the magnets in the pavement.

645
00:48:43,900 --> 00:48:52,700
And there's an experimental laser radar system and a millimeter wave radar, two different millimeter wave radar systems here.

646
00:48:53,140 --> 00:48:59,880
So in this case, we can do experiments to compare the performance of the different sensor systems and record the data and analyze the data.

647
00:48:59,881 --> 00:49:04,860
These prototypes are filled with high-tech wizardry

648
00:49:04,861 --> 00:49:08,300
that would be dramatically reduced in size in production models.

649
00:49:09,220 --> 00:49:12,760
So you could still carry all your luggage and golf clubs in the trunk.

650
00:49:12,860 --> 00:49:15,220
You wouldn't need to have all of these boxes there.

651
00:49:15,880 --> 00:49:20,420
The brains of this car is a single Pentium computer, which is inside this box.

652
00:49:20,940 --> 00:49:25,000
And it looks like a big box because it was designed as an industrial computer,

653
00:49:25,320 --> 00:49:29,120
but inside is just a single 166 megahertz Pentium.

654
00:49:29,121 --> 00:49:31,800
So by today's standards, it would be considered obsolete.

655
00:49:33,520 --> 00:49:38,560
Switching between normal driving and autopilot requires only a simple flip of a switch.

656
00:49:40,000 --> 00:49:44,360
We've done experiments on the transition between manual driving and automatic driving.

657
00:49:45,000 --> 00:49:50,080
Normally to do that, you'd have the driver push a button on the vehicle console or on the steering wheel

658
00:49:50,081 --> 00:49:53,560
to say that he's ready to give over control to the automatic system.

659
00:49:53,900 --> 00:49:57,880
That would have to be at a position like on the automated freeway on-ramp

660
00:49:57,881 --> 00:50:01,000
where the vehicle is actually capable of driving automatically.

661
00:50:03,100 --> 00:50:08,800
Stephen is the first to acknowledge that automated freeways will not be easily accepted by everyone.

662
00:50:10,000 --> 00:50:14,880
There will be certain people who are more inclined to like it, other people will be less inclined to like it.

663
00:50:15,200 --> 00:50:20,400
And this is an example of a technology that doesn't need to be used by everybody in order to have a big impact.

664
00:50:20,401 --> 00:50:26,680
It can have a lot of benefit for people who choose to use it and doesn't really affect the people who choose not to use it.

665
00:50:27,880 --> 00:50:33,120
In making his case for the automated system, Stephen points out something we seldom consider.

666
00:50:34,080 --> 00:50:42,380
A great part of the reason for heavy freeway traffic lies in the simple fact that people driving their own cars have a tendency to roam.

667
00:50:43,800 --> 00:50:49,240
Our freeway traffic is really limited by the ability of drivers to control the movement of their vehicles.

668
00:50:49,800 --> 00:50:54,520
The drivers need a lot of space on the side of the lanes because they can't steer that accurately.

669
00:50:54,521 --> 00:50:58,520
So a typical lane width is double the width of a typical passenger car.

670
00:50:58,521 --> 00:51:04,520
And we also have to leave lots of space between the vehicles just for people to feel comfortable when they're driving.

671
00:51:04,521 --> 00:51:09,520
That means that when you look from above at a freeway that's moving at full capacity,

672
00:51:09,521 --> 00:51:13,520
the vehicles are only occupying about 5% of the surface of that roadway.

673
00:51:13,521 --> 00:51:18,520
The other 95% of that road surface is the separation between the vehicles.

674
00:51:18,521 --> 00:51:23,520
So if we can use some good technology to make it possible for the vehicles to run closer together,

675
00:51:23,521 --> 00:51:30,520
we can increase the capacity of that highway and we don't need to pave as much space to provide the same transportation system capacity.

676
00:51:30,521 --> 00:51:39,520
That also means we can help improve safety and reduce the amount of pollution and the amount of energy consumption associated with highway transportation.

677
00:51:39,521 --> 00:51:44,520
It is a fact that automated highways do not have to be fiction.

678
00:51:44,521 --> 00:51:50,520
There appear, however, to be two obstacles blocking the path of such futuristic progress.

679
00:51:50,521 --> 00:51:56,900
funding, and the individual driver's willingness to give up control of his vehicle.

680
00:51:57,700 --> 00:52:04,400
If state highway departments could receive the same funds allocated to the visual effect sequences in Minority Report,

681
00:52:05,140 --> 00:52:07,780
the first issue could be easily resolved.

682
00:52:08,960 --> 00:52:14,560
As for the second obstacle, drivers just may prefer to ride their brakes for a lifetime

683
00:52:14,561 --> 00:52:17,360
rather than trust their lives to a computer.

684
00:52:17,361 --> 00:52:27,080
That's the great challenge that we have, is how do you get the freedom of a private automobile and yet the advantages of mass transit?

685
00:52:27,220 --> 00:52:31,300
And that's part of what we're trying to show with that technology, is that if you can combine these,

686
00:52:31,540 --> 00:52:37,840
you get people having that sense of personal control and freedom that the automobile gives you, and yet the efficiency of a mass transit system.

687
00:52:38,100 --> 00:52:40,840
So I think the combination of those two is actually quite likely.

688
00:52:40,841 --> 00:52:48,820
Minority Report not only set new goals for futuristic car makers,

689
00:52:48,821 --> 00:52:52,440
it did the same for law enforcement tools of the trade.

690
00:52:54,120 --> 00:53:00,680
Twenty years ago, cops and private eyes planted listening devices, or bugs, for the purpose of surveillance.

691
00:53:01,640 --> 00:53:06,840
Minority Report used spiders, spelled S-P-Y-D-E-R-S.

692
00:53:06,841 --> 00:53:13,840
Tiny mechanical bugs that could transmit picture and sound back to their police masters.

693
00:53:21,840 --> 00:53:29,840
Creation of the spiders involved the collaboration of computer programming wizards and production designers who really knew their bugs.

694
00:53:30,840 --> 00:53:35,840
In terms of both eavesdropping capabilities and the natural behavior of insects.

695
00:53:36,840 --> 00:53:42,960
We wanted to both be carefully walking and studying its environment

696
00:53:42,961 --> 00:53:46,060
as well as being aggressive and going after people.

697
00:53:46,061 --> 00:53:52,060
We did these motion studies and we were able to get both a real slow, careful walk

698
00:53:52,620 --> 00:53:55,000
where the spider is kind of studying its environment

699
00:53:55,001 --> 00:53:58,560
and a really aggressive walk working quite well.

700
00:53:59,540 --> 00:54:04,300
Once the personality of the spider was defined, then came the look.

701
00:54:04,301 --> 00:54:07,900
We did all sorts of studies of the surface.

702
00:54:08,200 --> 00:54:10,700
You know, this one's a little too dull, this one's a little too shiny,

703
00:54:11,200 --> 00:54:13,660
and this one, we've actually grunged it up.

704
00:54:14,040 --> 00:54:17,620
On the flippers, we actually did an iridescent moiré effect.

705
00:54:18,760 --> 00:54:23,760
The goal was to make the spiders look high-tech mechanical, yet alive.

706
00:54:25,080 --> 00:54:27,100
We put a little light source on top,

707
00:54:27,300 --> 00:54:29,660
and then there's a little light source in front of the spider.

708
00:54:29,661 --> 00:54:35,500
On the bottom of the spider, the little pulsing lights go through this iridescent spectrum,

709
00:54:35,860 --> 00:54:39,760
and I'd seen some deep-sea jellyfish at the Monterey Bay Aquarium.

710
00:54:40,260 --> 00:54:45,680
Its tentacles had this effect in it, and I thought it was just a perfect kind of effect to add to the spider

711
00:54:45,681 --> 00:54:49,680
and kind of make it look like it had some kind of heartbeat that was moving inside.

712
00:54:52,060 --> 00:54:57,680
The final result was a new movie character that sci-fi lovers are not likely to forget.

713
00:54:59,660 --> 00:55:06,660
You can see the integration in this shot, you know, both shadows on the ground and reflections

714
00:55:06,661 --> 00:55:11,421
of the spiders. All these little subtle details to, you know, really make it work.

715
00:55:11,422 --> 00:55:12,122
The spiders

716
00:55:12,123 --> 00:55:18,560
are just about to take off and they've all sprung up and they go dashing up the stairs.

717
00:55:18,561 --> 00:55:25,860
The concept of developing robot spider insects is definitely the stuff of Hollywood Imagineers.

718
00:55:25,861 --> 00:55:29,320
Could such devices actually be made and work?

719
00:55:30,560 --> 00:55:33,800
It's one thing to design a tiny micro-video camera,

720
00:55:34,340 --> 00:55:39,360
but quite another to make it move quickly and silently by remote control.

721
00:55:41,060 --> 00:55:43,900
For those who designed the make-believe spiders,

722
00:55:44,540 --> 00:55:48,680
the concept of real ones is, to say the least, intriguing.

723
00:55:49,680 --> 00:55:53,600
The future is always really interesting, but it's pretty scary, too.

724
00:55:53,601 --> 00:55:58,700
I think technology has a way of filtering out to the people.

725
00:55:58,920 --> 00:56:02,260
There's no way you can really control technology indefinitely.

726
00:56:02,920 --> 00:56:06,060
Boy, how do you figure out how to control something like that?

727
00:56:06,240 --> 00:56:07,440
That's kind of scary.

728
00:56:08,060 --> 00:56:13,260
Literally in the Afghan war, it already happened that we were sending little robots into the caves

729
00:56:13,261 --> 00:56:17,520
looking for Osama bin Laden with sensors able to detect the presence of human beings

730
00:56:17,521 --> 00:56:21,500
or bodies of human beings where nobody could get shot.

731
00:56:21,501 --> 00:56:30,213
You know, so in fact, that was the core idea that we were trying to show there, that there are going to be many situations in the future where you're going to try and

732
00:56:30,214 --> 00:56:32,680
be able to find people in deceptive situations.

733
00:56:33,040 --> 00:56:35,100
You wouldn't want to go in with guns blazing.

734
00:56:35,740 --> 00:56:37,800
And this would be an alternative technology.

735
00:56:38,000 --> 00:56:41,520
And in fact, what we showed on screen is pretty close to real today.

736
00:56:43,040 --> 00:56:49,680
If you've been following the flow of this program to this point, you have a pretty good idea what we're about to tell you.

737
00:56:49,681 --> 00:56:56,680
Tiny micro robotic insects designed to gather information in ways previously unimaginable

738
00:56:56,681 --> 00:56:59,680
do already exist.

739
00:56:59,681 --> 00:57:04,680
In laboratories of the University of California at Berkeley.

740
00:57:04,681 --> 00:57:11,680
There's basically only one difference between them and the tiny high-tech stars of Minority Report.

741
00:57:11,681 --> 00:57:16,680
They're not spiders. They're flies.

742
00:57:16,681 --> 00:57:23,680
At Berkeley, they're technically known as MFIs, micro-mechanical flying insects.

743
00:57:23,681 --> 00:57:26,680
But most call them robo-flies.

744
00:57:26,681 --> 00:57:31,680
They're being designed and built by UC Berkeley engineers.

745
00:57:31,681 --> 00:57:37,680
According to program director Ronald Furey, the design of the MFI is based on nature

746
00:57:37,681 --> 00:57:42,680
and mimics some of the key ingredients necessary to make a fly fly.

747
00:57:42,681 --> 00:57:48,640
There's a delayed stall so that a wing which is beating can have a high angle of attack

748
00:57:48,641 --> 00:57:49,640
and a high lift.

749
00:57:49,641 --> 00:57:54,300
The other thing that goes on is that the wing rotates at the bottom and the top of the stroke

750
00:57:54,301 --> 00:57:59,180
and that's like the backspin on a baseball and that gives more lift as well.

751
00:57:59,181 --> 00:58:05,640
In other words, a robofly can fly because it does the same thing a real fly does.

752
00:58:05,641 --> 00:58:09,720
Beat its wings like crazy and angle them just right.

753
00:58:09,721 --> 00:58:12,100
Purely by instinct.

754
00:58:12,101 --> 00:58:15,600
Of course, robo-flies, being machines, have no instinct.

755
00:58:16,540 --> 00:58:18,960
But they've got something just as powerful.

756
00:58:19,700 --> 00:58:23,460
A team of electrical engineers, biologists, and physicists

757
00:58:23,461 --> 00:58:29,940
who are well on the way to creating what could only be called techno-instinct.

758
00:58:31,800 --> 00:58:37,720
The project to develop robo-flies began in 1998 with U.S. military funding.

759
00:58:37,721 --> 00:58:47,980
The goal of the project was to develop a flying robot weighing less than a paper clip that could be used in search, rescue, monitoring and reconnaissance.

760
00:58:48,820 --> 00:58:51,960
Pretty much what the spiders could do in Minority Report.

761
00:58:53,880 --> 00:59:01,320
But in the real world, scientists felt that flying robots had advantages over miniaturized leg or wheeled ones.

762
00:59:02,480 --> 00:59:05,720
Rough terrain or even shag carpeting wouldn't pose a problem.

763
00:59:05,721 --> 00:59:08,900
and they can move much more quickly.

764
00:59:10,260 --> 00:59:13,200
The challenge for engineers is enormous.

765
00:59:14,120 --> 00:59:18,060
The problem is that we can't just take an off-the-shelf little miniature gearbox

766
00:59:18,061 --> 00:59:20,180
and put together a fly.

767
00:59:20,181 --> 00:59:26,440
The gearboxes in these flies are incredibly amazing engineering feats

768
00:59:26,441 --> 00:59:28,880
that no one really understands.

769
00:59:29,640 --> 00:59:32,200
It's something in the size of like a cubic millimeter,

770
00:59:32,201 --> 00:59:38,760
which converts from the main muscle motion of the fly into this precise motion of the wing.

771
00:59:40,300 --> 00:59:47,680
Since 1998, Daring and his team have successfully miniaturized most pieces required for the MFI.

772
00:59:49,520 --> 00:59:54,380
One of the greatest achievements was the invention of a wing-drive thorax

773
00:59:54,381 --> 00:59:57,580
composed of micro-thin sheets of stainless steel

774
00:59:57,581 --> 01:00:02,400
that when cut and folded into beams turn out to be very strong.

775
01:00:04,460 --> 01:00:08,300
Two hinged beams are attached as struts to each wing

776
01:00:08,301 --> 01:00:10,960
with a tiny electric motor driving them.

777
01:00:11,700 --> 01:00:14,000
When they move together, the wing flaps.

778
01:00:14,560 --> 01:00:17,080
When they move out of sink, the wing rotates.

779
01:00:18,140 --> 01:00:22,380
The wings are approximately 1 20th the thickness of a sheet of paper

780
01:00:22,381 --> 01:00:24,560
made of lightweight polyester.

781
01:00:24,561 --> 01:00:29,440
We've got a device now which has got a 25 millimeter wingspan.

782
01:00:29,600 --> 01:00:34,220
So this device here has got a wing which is about a centimeter long

783
01:00:34,221 --> 01:00:36,180
and then about a half centimeter body length.

784
01:00:36,760 --> 01:00:41,220
And so we've shrunk down from original giant paper models and metal models

785
01:00:41,221 --> 01:00:43,500
to what the final size device is.

786
01:00:44,320 --> 01:00:48,580
Now the Berkeley Group is working on perfecting the lightweight power supply.

787
01:00:49,580 --> 01:00:53,320
Solar panels integrated into the RoboFly's tripod legs.

788
01:00:54,560 --> 01:00:57,680
Next will come a gyroscope to ensure level flight.

789
01:00:59,500 --> 01:01:05,220
An incredibly small microprocessor with an operating system called TinyOS

790
01:01:05,221 --> 01:01:10,200
has already been developed at UC Berkeley for devices of this size.

791
01:01:11,420 --> 01:01:16,380
The reason we make these little robots insect-like is because what we've seen is, A, they work.

792
01:01:16,760 --> 01:01:17,900
Insects do get around.

793
01:01:18,240 --> 01:01:20,260
And they have a variety of mechanisms.

794
01:01:20,261 --> 01:01:23,740
For example, I just saw one that looks like a millipede.

795
01:01:23,741 --> 01:01:26,040
Great for walking over rubble.

796
01:01:26,860 --> 01:01:29,760
Millions of little eggs, or in this case, thousands of little eggs,

797
01:01:30,140 --> 01:01:31,800
that can wander over rubble.

798
01:01:33,120 --> 01:01:36,920
Mass production of robo-flies may still be years away,

799
01:01:37,360 --> 01:01:42,040
but the practical application possibilities are virtually limitless.

800
01:01:43,000 --> 01:01:47,460
We could start out with entertainment applications, make a great toy for kids.

801
01:01:48,080 --> 01:01:49,920
As the capability starts getting better,

802
01:01:49,921 --> 01:01:54,100
we could think about civilian applications, disaster scenarios,

803
01:01:54,240 --> 01:01:57,780
search and rescue, finding lost people, things like that.

804
01:01:58,160 --> 01:02:02,760
More speculative, if you can really get the costs down and really mass-produce these,

805
01:02:03,120 --> 01:02:04,920
you can think of agricultural applications.

806
01:02:05,440 --> 01:02:09,600
Instead of using insecticides, send out robots to kill just the pest species

807
01:02:09,601 --> 01:02:12,460
and not harm the helpful species.

808
01:02:13,680 --> 01:02:15,860
One place we'll certainly use it is in the media.

809
01:02:16,260 --> 01:02:17,420
You can see it in the news.

810
01:02:17,421 --> 01:02:24,220
I mean, I would not be at all surprised within fairly short order that we will see many video cameras hung on these little robot.

811
01:02:24,640 --> 01:02:28,920
What will look like almost little helicopters that will be a few inches long with a little camera,

812
01:02:29,200 --> 01:02:36,220
and you can just see at an accident site or some news story, you'll see a little swarm of these overhead taking pictures from many different angles.

813
01:02:36,380 --> 01:02:40,040
And, you know, a news station might send 25 of these to orbit over a site.

814
01:02:40,120 --> 01:02:44,260
Somebody else will have theirs, and they'll be all jostling to get the right shot.

815
01:02:44,261 --> 01:02:47,640
they will be relatively cheap and semi-disposable.

816
01:02:49,920 --> 01:02:53,460
And what does the head spider creator from Minority Report

817
01:02:53,461 --> 01:02:55,740
think about his real-life competition?

818
01:02:56,920 --> 01:02:58,500
Hats off to them, you know.

819
01:02:58,680 --> 01:03:00,620
We thought what we were doing was complicated.

820
01:03:00,920 --> 01:03:04,040
If they can make it for real, wow, I'm impressed.

821
01:03:04,700 --> 01:03:07,980
The lightsabers, wielded by actors in the Star Wars films,

822
01:03:08,120 --> 01:03:10,360
were actually lightweight rods made from aluminum.

823
01:03:10,940 --> 01:03:13,060
Greatest Movie Gadgets will return in a moment.

824
01:03:14,260 --> 01:03:16,380
Okay, and I put this in my eye.

825
01:03:16,540 --> 01:03:17,240
Yeah, put it in your eye.

826
01:03:17,460 --> 01:03:18,160
This is some spy.

827
01:03:18,280 --> 01:03:20,240
In the hit movie, Eye Spy,

828
01:03:20,560 --> 01:03:25,160
Eddie Murphy got a kick out of his first exposure to polyalloy transducers.

829
01:03:25,260 --> 01:03:25,960
Can you see anything?

830
01:03:26,540 --> 01:03:30,600
Contact lenses designed to be worn in the eyes of two different people.

831
01:03:30,960 --> 01:03:32,700
One wears the transmitter lens.

832
01:03:32,860 --> 01:03:34,480
The other, the receiver.

833
01:03:34,560 --> 01:03:36,400
I see me looking at you, looking at me, and I see me.

834
01:03:36,840 --> 01:03:38,480
Hey, get a Robson.

835
01:03:38,900 --> 01:03:40,680
Whatever the first person looks at,

836
01:03:40,780 --> 01:03:42,560
the second one sees.

837
01:03:42,561 --> 01:03:44,560
Oh, s***, I like this.

838
01:03:44,840 --> 01:03:48,240
Gadgets like these, made basically for movie star spies,

839
01:03:48,920 --> 01:03:51,040
have been audience favorites for years.

840
01:03:53,360 --> 01:03:55,960
Poly-alloy transducers may be years away,

841
01:03:56,540 --> 01:04:02,040
but contemporary spies have other space-age gadgetry already working in the field.

842
01:04:03,100 --> 01:04:08,900
The size and configuration of cameras being able to snoop and transmit without being detected

843
01:04:08,901 --> 01:04:11,440
is nothing short of mind-boggling.

844
01:04:12,560 --> 01:04:21,560
To check out the latest and greatest, we headed to the Bolide Technology Group store in California, one of the nation's largest.

845
01:04:22,680 --> 01:04:26,180
Here, they not only sell state-of-the-art spy tools.

846
01:04:27,280 --> 01:04:29,300
If they don't exist, they make them.

847
01:04:30,540 --> 01:04:38,780
This technician is wiring up a light fixture so that it's not only good as a reading lamp, but as a seeing lamp.

848
01:04:38,781 --> 01:04:41,560
The tiny camera hidden inside.

849
01:04:43,380 --> 01:04:47,420
If Bull-Eyed Technology had a slogan, it would probably be,

850
01:04:47,880 --> 01:04:50,920
if you can bring it in the door, we can put a camera in it.

851
01:04:51,640 --> 01:04:53,460
Check out this baseball cap.

852
01:04:54,200 --> 01:04:55,020
Where's the camera?

853
01:04:55,780 --> 01:04:58,040
In the middle of the A in Excalibur.

854
01:04:59,920 --> 01:05:01,400
How about this day timer?

855
01:05:02,160 --> 01:05:07,160
The tiny pinhole in the spine wasn't caused by an errant flying paperclip.

856
01:05:07,161 --> 01:05:13,060
Yep, the camera looks out through it.

857
01:05:13,061 --> 01:05:19,240
This belt-snap water bottle not only holds a camera, but a transmitter which can send

858
01:05:19,241 --> 01:05:23,620
the video signal to a nearby tape recorder.

859
01:05:23,621 --> 01:05:25,920
The same with this clock radio.

860
01:05:25,921 --> 01:05:31,240
The camera hole right here.

861
01:05:31,241 --> 01:05:37,440
Any one of these tiny cameras, priced from $50 to $200, can be mounted into basically

862
01:05:37,441 --> 01:05:38,440
anything.

863
01:05:38,441 --> 01:05:44,860
They may all look pretty much the same to the average gadget guy, but to Bolide Technology

864
01:05:44,861 --> 01:05:49,700
account executive Frank Comfort, they all have their specific purposes.

865
01:05:49,701 --> 01:05:55,120
Here we have a display of various board cameras and enclosed board cameras.

866
01:05:55,121 --> 01:05:58,680
You can see some of the different picture qualities that you get.

867
01:05:58,681 --> 01:06:00,340
Some have real wide-angle pictures.

868
01:06:01,140 --> 01:06:04,560
Some have narrower-angle pictures.

869
01:06:04,860 --> 01:06:05,560
Some are color.

870
01:06:05,660 --> 01:06:06,580
Some are black and white.

871
01:06:06,860 --> 01:06:09,320
There's a lot of variety, a lot of different features that are available.

872
01:06:10,880 --> 01:06:15,820
Okay, now that you've seen the big cameras, it's time to look at the small ones.

873
01:06:16,680 --> 01:06:17,600
You think we're kidding?

874
01:06:18,200 --> 01:06:18,980
We're not.

875
01:06:19,840 --> 01:06:22,200
What we're looking at here is a covert pen camera.

876
01:06:22,340 --> 01:06:27,120
The actual camera part is the small circle right at the top of the pen clip itself.

877
01:06:27,121 --> 01:06:31,400
These are commonly used by private investigators and people in law enforcement.

878
01:06:31,780 --> 01:06:38,120
They'll connect this device to a small transmitter or recorder located elsewhere on their body.

879
01:06:38,840 --> 01:06:41,440
You say you never wear a pen in your shirt pocket?

880
01:06:41,980 --> 01:06:42,720
No problem.

881
01:06:43,380 --> 01:06:45,320
Just change the buttons on your shirt.

882
01:06:46,220 --> 01:06:48,040
This is another body-worn camera.

883
01:06:48,200 --> 01:06:51,500
It's actually installed in your shirt in place of one of the regular buttons.

884
01:06:52,140 --> 01:06:56,360
As part of a set, you would swap all the different buttons out so that they match.

885
01:06:56,361 --> 01:07:00,540
what you see by moving your body, what you get there on screen.

886
01:07:03,360 --> 01:07:07,400
And for all of you who yearn for A.D. Murphy's contact lens cameras,

887
01:07:08,540 --> 01:07:10,500
well, they're not too far off.

888
01:07:11,860 --> 01:07:15,000
Depending on your mood, you can choose to look studious,

889
01:07:15,360 --> 01:07:18,580
wearing formal glasses with a camera hidden in the nose bridge,

890
01:07:19,140 --> 01:07:22,280
or just plain cool.

891
01:07:23,280 --> 01:07:24,880
The camera's in the same place,

892
01:07:25,740 --> 01:07:29,240
just even harder to see through these smoke-gray plastic lenses.

893
01:07:31,380 --> 01:07:35,400
Now, if these glasses could only give the wearer X-ray vision.

894
01:07:36,640 --> 01:07:38,520
Two targets, rooftop. North side.

895
01:07:38,760 --> 01:07:40,980
Understood. Two targets, rooftop. Maintain visual, please.

896
01:07:40,981 --> 01:07:42,500
You have something they want!

897
01:07:42,620 --> 01:07:43,580
I don't have anything!

898
01:07:43,920 --> 01:07:47,780
In the movie Enemy of the State, Will Smith is an actor on the run.

899
01:07:47,800 --> 01:07:49,820
If you come near either one of us, I'm going to kill you.

900
01:07:50,080 --> 01:07:50,980
Get rid of your watch!

901
01:07:51,300 --> 01:07:52,020
Target's on the move.

902
01:07:52,280 --> 01:07:55,960
His location is being tracked by satellite,

903
01:07:56,840 --> 01:07:59,480
receiving a signal from a tiny transmitter he's wearing.

904
01:08:01,020 --> 01:08:03,000
Okay, gentlemen, we're back online.

905
01:08:03,360 --> 01:08:05,020
Okay, wireframe download complete.

906
01:08:05,640 --> 01:08:08,000
All right, we have a tracer in the stairwell on 20.

907
01:08:08,080 --> 01:08:08,860
Traveling down.

908
01:08:09,200 --> 01:08:11,140
19, 18.

909
01:08:11,560 --> 01:08:13,560
Computers record his every move.

910
01:08:13,940 --> 01:08:16,820
In effect, seeing right through objects.

