you already have what amount to lanes in the air already for aircraft.

1145
01:23:58,301 --> 01:24:02,300
You know, we have literally highways in the sky that planes follow.

1146
01:24:02,540 --> 01:24:06,700
and you are creating that visually on a screen using electronic communications.

1147
01:24:07,020 --> 01:24:08,580
That's the kind of thing that would be happening here.

1148
01:24:08,660 --> 01:24:12,660
You would be creating those kinds of lanes that the driver would experience,

1149
01:24:12,760 --> 01:24:17,060
but you wouldn't see lane markers and that sort of thing, or exit markers floating in the air.

1150
01:24:17,260 --> 01:24:19,340
It would all appear virtually on your screen.

1151
01:24:21,000 --> 01:24:25,260
But when such dreams come true, prepare for the nightmares.

1152
01:24:26,480 --> 01:24:31,240
The biggest issue in things like flying cars is the complete freedom to move anywhere.

1153
01:24:31,241 --> 01:24:36,360
Imagine when 3,000 flying cars descend on Yosemite Valley on a beautiful afternoon.

1154
01:24:36,640 --> 01:24:38,360
Think about the issues of safety.

1155
01:24:38,880 --> 01:24:40,920
We already have problems of drunken drivers.

1156
01:24:41,060 --> 01:24:43,980
Imagine a drunken driver behind the wheel of a flying car.

1157
01:24:44,380 --> 01:24:45,740
Boy, that's going to be hard to pull off.

1158
01:24:46,800 --> 01:24:50,640
We know that automated highways are being tested near San Diego.

1159
01:24:51,720 --> 01:24:59,660
But is it even conceivable to think that maglev freeways, as seen in Minority Report, could be built in the United States?

1160
01:25:01,240 --> 01:25:05,200
It's, first of all, extremely expensive, you know, because you can't tear down a lot of stuff.

1161
01:25:05,260 --> 01:25:09,030
When you're building in a new environment that isn't yet well-developed, i.e.

1162
01:25:09,031 --> 01:25:12,360
the line from, say, Shanghai Airport to downtown, it's easier to do.

1163
01:25:12,640 --> 01:25:19,492
But when you're having to deal with the fact that you've got the White House, the Capitol, old neighborhoods and so on that you want to preserve, it's extremely

1164
01:25:19,493 --> 01:25:20,193
expensive.

1165
01:25:20,320 --> 01:25:30,800
Secondly, launching such a major undertaking in any large city has huge political implications because there's massive disruptions, huge costs involved and so on.

1166
01:25:30,801 --> 01:25:35,060
Now, Washington might be one of the few places where you might get away with that,

1167
01:25:35,280 --> 01:25:39,323
because Washington in some ways becomes a demonstration for the world of U.S.

1168
01:25:39,324 --> 01:25:40,024
capability.

1169
01:25:41,200 --> 01:25:46,540
We've seen how miniature eavesdropping devices like robo-flies may soon be a reality.

1170
01:25:47,580 --> 01:25:51,960
But will their use be blocked by concerns about the violation of privacy?

1171
01:25:53,440 --> 01:25:59,320
I think the reality is that we're moving into a world where privacy is going to be very hard to achieve.

1172
01:25:59,321 --> 01:26:01,880
One of my colleagues, Jay Ogilvie, put it, I think, beautifully.

1173
01:26:02,060 --> 01:26:04,480
You know, Andy Warhol talked about 15 minutes of fame.

1174
01:26:04,820 --> 01:26:06,520
No, no, it's going to be 15 minutes of privacy.

1175
01:26:07,300 --> 01:26:09,200
We'll all be famous, too famous.

1176
01:26:09,840 --> 01:26:11,560
We will be widely observed.

1177
01:26:11,800 --> 01:26:13,300
Look, we've already seen it.

1178
01:26:13,680 --> 01:26:17,340
That is how many cameras are around our society already today.

1179
01:26:17,820 --> 01:26:20,920
The average American is on video 36 times a day.

1180
01:26:21,640 --> 01:26:24,680
You know, when you go to the bank teller machine, when you walked into the supermarket,

1181
01:26:24,681 --> 01:26:29,420
when you went into your office building, when you paid your toll at the bridge, and so on.

1182
01:26:29,540 --> 01:26:31,980
Every time you did that, you were on video.

1183
01:26:32,540 --> 01:26:37,640
And so I think that's already the reality, that we're moving into a world where we're under constant observation.

1184
01:26:39,660 --> 01:26:44,720
And what kind of new developments can we expect in the realm of personal communication devices?

1185
01:26:46,960 --> 01:26:51,580
We're getting down to kind of the optimal size that the technology permits.

1186
01:26:51,581 --> 01:26:53,000
But there'll be many different forms.

1187
01:26:53,001 --> 01:26:55,660
you can imagine it as a pin you might wear on your chest.

1188
01:26:55,840 --> 01:26:57,920
It might be the wrist communicator of Dick Tracy.

1189
01:26:58,340 --> 01:27:00,420
It might be something you wear over your ear.

1190
01:27:00,880 --> 01:27:02,900
It might be incorporated into glasses.

1191
01:27:03,080 --> 01:27:04,160
It might be part of a hat.

1192
01:27:04,380 --> 01:27:05,760
It might be embedded in clothing.

1193
01:27:06,280 --> 01:27:10,020
So we will see many different kinds of form factors for the technology,

1194
01:27:10,320 --> 01:27:12,660
but it won't get a lot smaller than it is right now.

1195
01:27:14,060 --> 01:27:19,720
And could portable communication devices evolve into video as well as audio transmission?

1196
01:27:19,721 --> 01:27:27,540
Well, interestingly enough, you know, video conferencing has never taken off, and video phones have never taken off.

1197
01:27:27,920 --> 01:27:31,360
And I think the main reason is that most people don't actually want to be seen.

1198
01:27:31,920 --> 01:27:34,980
You know, imagine answering the phone in your underwear in the morning, right?

1199
01:27:35,400 --> 01:27:37,080
You know, and suddenly you're on camera.

1200
01:27:38,200 --> 01:27:39,720
You want to avoid that.

1201
01:27:39,860 --> 01:27:43,220
And in fact, the truth is that most people don't really want to be seen.

1202
01:27:43,221 --> 01:27:51,220
In a fascinating twist, Peter Schwartz foresees a day when the biggest challenge of communication

1203
01:27:51,221 --> 01:27:56,220
technology may be figuring out how to become unreachable.

1204
01:27:56,221 --> 01:28:02,740
We will see an enormous amount of connection among various kinds of technology and among

1205
01:28:02,741 --> 01:28:04,740
people in a variety of ways.

1206
01:28:04,741 --> 01:28:09,460
So while the scale may not be shrinking, we will see many different form factors and in

1207
01:28:09,461 --> 01:28:11,540
a variety of different situations.

1208
01:28:11,541 --> 01:28:13,660
The reality is that we will always be connected.

1209
01:28:13,900 --> 01:28:14,880
That's the big change.

1210
01:28:15,400 --> 01:28:16,680
We will always be connected.

1211
01:28:16,980 --> 01:28:19,100
And you will actively have to disconnect.

1212
01:28:20,360 --> 01:28:25,780
And so that's going to have a very, very large change in society that flows from that.

1213
01:28:28,600 --> 01:28:30,800
Finally, what about T-rays?

1214
01:28:32,100 --> 01:28:35,680
Peter Schwartz is very enthusiastic about this new technology.

1215
01:28:36,680 --> 01:28:39,580
And not only for medical and security applications.

1216
01:28:39,581 --> 01:28:49,600
A good example is in buildings finding pipes and wiring and so on is actually very hard because the plans are often gone, particularly in old buildings.

1217
01:28:50,000 --> 01:28:56,700
This is the kind of device you might use if you're going to remodel an old office building and you want to find out where the old service is in the building.

1218
01:28:56,920 --> 01:29:03,720
You can look through the walls and say, ah, that's where the pipes are, that's where the wiring is, and so on, and do a better job in the construction process.

1219
01:29:03,721 --> 01:29:06,980
predicting the future of technology

1220
01:29:06,981 --> 01:29:10,960
may be as difficult as predicting which Hollywood gadget-driven movie

1221
01:29:10,961 --> 01:29:12,700
will do well at the box office.

1222
01:29:14,780 --> 01:29:18,680
Right now, high-tech equals high-profit moviemaking.

1223
01:29:19,220 --> 01:29:21,120
It is fascinating, however,

1224
01:29:21,440 --> 01:29:26,400
to understand that what we perceive as innovative ideas on the silver screen

1225
01:29:26,401 --> 01:29:30,100
are often preceded by real-world research

1226
01:29:30,101 --> 01:29:32,980
that filmmakers may or may not know about.

1227
01:29:33,720 --> 01:29:40,320
The relationship between artists and scientists has been one of mutual inspiration.

1228
01:29:41,900 --> 01:29:45,300
Hopefully, that relationship will never change.

